out  the tequila  mark you do know that alcohol is  classified as a group one carcinogen and  is broken down  into a known toxin acetaldehyde  right i am so  over this elitist anti-chemical wellness  discourse  chemicals are in everything and natural  is not always  better as an example like here's a fun  little factoid to bust out at your next  zoom dinner party  we have artificial flavors like vanilla  that are made in a lab  and while they're deemed safe by the fda  they are  man-made chemicals synthesized in a lab  meanwhile natural vanilla extract can  not only  come from the vanilla bean but also from  get ready for this folks beaver's anal  glands  what yup yup yup  so you tell me which one sounds more  like  food to you we can communicate our food  preferences without moralizing  others and suggesting that certain foods  are poison  or toxic or just downright and edible  because they've been processed in some  sort of way  oh okay buy you comfort food what would  i eat  once in a while i love pasta so i might  have you know lentil pasta chickpea  pasta maybe soba noodles buckwheat  noodles  and i'll make a little pasta dish which  i really love okay so i am a huge fan  of bean based pastas because it's a  great source of protein and fiber  and is appropriate for those who may  need to eat a gluten-free diet  but based on his own arbitrary pegan  rules  this comfort food would be a major no-no  the ingredients in banza for example are  chickpeas pea protein tapioca starch and  xanthan gum last time i checked all of  those things are discouraged when you  eat  pegan i mean xanthan gum specifically i  would think mark would call out as some  sort of new age  poison because it is derived from sugar  and a lot of these gums and emulsifiers  often get demonized in wellness circles  for  their alleged role in poor digestion and  weight gain i've actually got a whole  blog post  outlining the faulty mice research that  this assertion is based on  but just know that 